WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.280 --> 00:00:03.470
- [Devin] Are NFTs a scam or
are they the next big thing?

00:00:03.470 --> 00:00:06.750
Legally speaking, it's a question
between Uber and Theranos.

00:00:06.750 --> 00:00:08.940
Uber was able to sidestep regulation

00:00:08.940 --> 00:00:10.100
to become a billion dollar company,

00:00:10.100 --> 00:00:13.160
and basically is interwoven
into the fabric of society.

00:00:13.160 --> 00:00:16.060
Whereas Theranos tried to
fake it until they made it,

00:00:16.060 --> 00:00:18.560
but they ran up against
physics and biology

00:00:18.560 --> 00:00:20.750
and were just a giant fraud.

00:00:20.750 --> 00:00:24.160
NFTs are probably somewhere in the middle.

00:00:24.160 --> 00:00:25.350
They're certainly not unicorns

00:00:25.350 --> 00:00:27.230
that are going to solve all problems,

00:00:27.230 --> 00:00:30.200
but they're probably not
all completely useless.

00:00:30.200 --> 00:00:33.480
And either way they raise
fundamental issues of law.

00:00:33.480 --> 00:00:35.960
So today we're going to
cover only the legal problems

00:00:35.960 --> 00:00:37.500
associated with NFTs.

00:00:37.500 --> 00:00:39.880
Others have explained
the non-legal issues.

00:00:39.880 --> 00:00:41.870
For example, Dan Olson of Folding Ideas

00:00:41.870 --> 00:00:44.270
did a fantastic video
called The Line Goes Up,

00:00:44.270 --> 00:00:45.770
which is not to say that the technology

00:00:45.770 --> 00:00:47.440
can't overcome these problems,

00:00:47.440 --> 00:00:50.490
but right now there are some
fundamental issues of law

00:00:50.490 --> 00:00:52.570
with the way that NFTs are used now.

00:00:52.570 --> 00:00:55.420
And I see three main
categories of issues with NFTs,

00:00:55.420 --> 00:00:57.700
on-chain problems, off-chain problems,

00:00:57.700 --> 00:01:01.700
and what I call innovations so
mundane, no one should care.

00:01:01.700 --> 00:01:03.670
Now, to get to those issues with NFTs,

00:01:03.670 --> 00:01:06.120
we first have to define what an NFT is

00:01:06.120 --> 00:01:08.740
because I think almost
everyone gets it wrong.

00:01:08.740 --> 00:01:11.060
An NFT is a Non Fungible Token.

00:01:11.060 --> 00:01:13.870
It is an entry on a decentralized ledger,

00:01:13.870 --> 00:01:14.753
called a blockchain.

00:01:14.753 --> 00:01:17.530
A blockchain is a permanent
unchangeable digital ledger

00:01:17.530 --> 00:01:19.300
that's used to record transactions

00:01:19.300 --> 00:01:21.230
in blocks of computer code

00:01:21.230 --> 00:01:23.590
that are timestamped and linked together.

00:01:23.590 --> 00:01:25.780
The code will reveal the
history of a digital asset,

00:01:25.780 --> 00:01:26.613
where it originated,

00:01:26.613 --> 00:01:28.660
who purchased it and any
subsequent transfers.

00:01:28.660 --> 00:01:29.650
And in the digital world,

00:01:29.650 --> 00:01:32.870
the token can be assigned
specific uses and properties.

00:01:32.870 --> 00:01:35.360
Now, it's non fungible because
it is said to be unique.

00:01:35.360 --> 00:01:37.280
Unlike something like Bitcoin,

00:01:37.280 --> 00:01:38.740
where every single Bitcoin

00:01:38.740 --> 00:01:40.470
is interchangeable with another one,

00:01:40.470 --> 00:01:42.230
it's like $1 bill.

00:01:42.230 --> 00:01:44.120
Every $1 bill that you possess

00:01:44.120 --> 00:01:45.680
effectively does the exact same thing

00:01:45.680 --> 00:01:46.750
as another dollar bill.

00:01:46.750 --> 00:01:48.150
Same with a Bitcoin.

00:01:48.150 --> 00:01:50.530
However, an NFT is said to be unique,

00:01:50.530 --> 00:01:53.080
so they are not fungible with each other.

00:01:53.080 --> 00:01:53.913
But at this point,

00:01:53.913 --> 00:01:55.590
let me turn it over to someone
more technically minded,

00:01:55.590 --> 00:01:56.860
my friend Steve Mould,

00:01:56.860 --> 00:01:59.030
to explain how an NFT actually operates

00:01:59.030 --> 00:02:00.600
because it's illuminating.

00:02:00.600 --> 00:02:03.030
So Steve, how does an NFT work?

00:02:03.030 --> 00:02:06.560
- NFTs work in a very
similar way to Bitcoins.

00:02:06.560 --> 00:02:10.330
We track who owns what Bitcoin
on a distributed ledger,

00:02:10.330 --> 00:02:11.240
as you mentioned.

00:02:11.240 --> 00:02:12.770
In the case of Bitcoin,

00:02:12.770 --> 00:02:16.530
that distributed ledger is
called the Bitcoin Blockchain,

00:02:16.530 --> 00:02:19.560
but NFTs exist on a different blockchain,

00:02:19.560 --> 00:02:22.220
typically the Ethereum Blockchain.

00:02:22.220 --> 00:02:24.860
The difference between
Bitcoin and Ethereum

00:02:24.860 --> 00:02:27.950
is that the Ethereum
blockchain is programmable.

00:02:27.950 --> 00:02:30.590
You can write programs and publish them

00:02:30.590 --> 00:02:32.210
to the Ethereum Blockchain.

00:02:32.210 --> 00:02:35.120
These aren't programs that you
would run on your computer,

00:02:35.120 --> 00:02:37.380
these programs run on the blockchain

00:02:37.380 --> 00:02:40.540
and they interact with
transactions on the blockchain.

00:02:40.540 --> 00:02:41.840
For example,

00:02:41.840 --> 00:02:44.240
you could write a program and publish it

00:02:44.240 --> 00:02:45.510
to the Ethereum Blockchain

00:02:45.510 --> 00:02:49.400
that launches a new
cryptocurrency to rival Bitcoin.

00:02:49.400 --> 00:02:51.930
But it's not just limited
to cryptocurrency,

00:02:51.930 --> 00:02:54.050
you could use the Ethereum
programming language

00:02:54.050 --> 00:02:55.670
to do all sorts of things,

00:02:55.670 --> 00:02:58.840
including for example, making NFTs.

00:02:58.840 --> 00:03:01.230
These programs are called smart contracts,

00:03:01.230 --> 00:03:03.840
which is misleading because look,

00:03:03.840 --> 00:03:05.630
here's an example of a smart contract.

00:03:05.630 --> 00:03:07.510
There's no legal language in there.

00:03:07.510 --> 00:03:10.130
It doesn't function as a legal document.

00:03:10.130 --> 00:03:10.963
Though, I can see

00:03:10.963 --> 00:03:12.830
why they wanted to call
them smart contract

00:03:12.830 --> 00:03:16.830
because they enforce transactional
rules on the blockchain.

00:03:16.830 --> 00:03:18.380
Just like normal software,

00:03:18.380 --> 00:03:21.220
once you publish a smart
contract to the blockchain,

00:03:21.220 --> 00:03:24.430
you can interact with it
by sending it commands.

00:03:24.430 --> 00:03:25.263
For example,

00:03:25.263 --> 00:03:28.910
you could send a command
telling it to mint a new NFT,

00:03:28.910 --> 00:03:32.850
or you could send a command
to change the owner of an NFT.

00:03:32.850 --> 00:03:33.840
Smart contracts can be used

00:03:33.840 --> 00:03:36.870
for much more than just
cryptocurrency and NFTs.

00:03:36.870 --> 00:03:37.703
For example,

00:03:37.703 --> 00:03:40.440
you could write a smart
contract that act as an escrow

00:03:40.440 --> 00:03:43.230
between people buying and selling NFTs.

00:03:43.230 --> 00:03:45.760
The definition of an NFT is quite strict.

00:03:45.760 --> 00:03:49.560
By consensus, we've agreed
that if a smart contract

00:03:49.560 --> 00:03:52.170
behaves in certain specific ways,

00:03:52.170 --> 00:03:56.450
as defined by a standard
called ERC721 and one other,

00:03:56.450 --> 00:03:59.830
then we call that an NFT smart contract.

00:03:59.830 --> 00:04:03.010
So how does all this
relate to digital art?

00:04:03.010 --> 00:04:04.060
Well, let's have a look

00:04:04.060 --> 00:04:07.140
at the most expensive Bored Ape ever sold,

00:04:07.140 --> 00:04:10.390
that's Bored Ape number 2087.

00:04:10.390 --> 00:04:12.250
Let's head over to Etherscan,

00:04:12.250 --> 00:04:14.960
a website for exploring
the Ethereum Blockchain.

00:04:14.960 --> 00:04:17.520
This is the address of the smart contract

00:04:17.520 --> 00:04:20.160
that mints Bored Ape NFTs.

00:04:20.160 --> 00:04:24.520
ERC721 contracts can be
queried in certain ways.

00:04:24.520 --> 00:04:25.353
For example,

00:04:25.353 --> 00:04:27.700
I can use the owner of query

00:04:27.700 --> 00:04:30.910
to find out who owns a particular NFT.

00:04:30.910 --> 00:04:35.450
So Bored Ape 2087 is owned by this person.

00:04:35.450 --> 00:04:37.710
The ERC721 standard also includes

00:04:37.710 --> 00:04:40.040
something called the token URI.

00:04:40.040 --> 00:04:43.370
If I put 2087 in here, I get this.

00:04:43.370 --> 00:04:47.420
This is a URI on the public
internet for anyone to see.

00:04:47.420 --> 00:04:48.570
It's an IPFS address,

00:04:48.570 --> 00:04:51.030
so let's head over to the Brave Browser

00:04:51.030 --> 00:04:52.400
and see where that takes us.

00:04:52.400 --> 00:04:55.000
This is some machine readable JSON code,

00:04:55.000 --> 00:04:57.430
but let's make it a bit
easier to read for humans.

00:04:57.430 --> 00:05:01.380
And look, you can see that's
URI for the ape image itself.

00:05:01.380 --> 00:05:03.500
And here are some attributes of the ape.

00:05:03.500 --> 00:05:06.470
For example, its mouth is bored cigarette.

00:05:06.470 --> 00:05:07.920
The background is purple.

00:05:07.920 --> 00:05:09.250
The fur is trippy.

00:05:09.250 --> 00:05:10.800
The eyes are angry.

00:05:10.800 --> 00:05:12.370
Let's grab this image URI

00:05:12.370 --> 00:05:14.330
and stick it back into the Brave Browser.

00:05:14.330 --> 00:05:15.420
And there it is.

00:05:15.420 --> 00:05:18.693
So when you buy an NFT that's
linked to a digital artwork,

00:05:18.693 --> 00:05:23.130
you are buying a unique location
on the Ethereum Blockchain,

00:05:23.130 --> 00:05:27.280
that points to a text file on the internet

00:05:27.280 --> 00:05:28.410
for everyone to see.

00:05:28.410 --> 00:05:31.000
Inside that text file is another URI

00:05:31.000 --> 00:05:32.780
that points to the image itself,

00:05:32.780 --> 00:05:35.850
again on the internet for everyone to see.

00:05:35.850 --> 00:05:36.683
Believe me,

00:05:36.683 --> 00:05:39.860
there are a bunch of
weird technical details

00:05:39.860 --> 00:05:41.130
that need to be talked about,

00:05:41.130 --> 00:05:42.780
associated with NFTs,

00:05:42.780 --> 00:05:44.730
I cover all of that over on my channel,

00:05:44.730 --> 00:05:45.950
but the bottom line is,

00:05:45.950 --> 00:05:47.143
when you buy crypto art,

00:05:47.143 --> 00:05:50.640
you are buying a line
in a tamper-proof ledger

00:05:50.640 --> 00:05:54.690
that points to a publicly
hosted image file.

00:05:54.690 --> 00:05:56.530
- So at the end of the day, to summarize,

00:05:56.530 --> 00:06:00.730
I think the best analogy for
an NFT is not digital property,

00:06:00.730 --> 00:06:02.960
as a lot of people like to claim.

00:06:02.960 --> 00:06:06.070
I think the best analogy
for what an NFT actually is,

00:06:06.070 --> 00:06:07.590
is a receipt.

00:06:07.590 --> 00:06:10.570
It is a receipt of some transaction,

00:06:10.570 --> 00:06:13.090
a buyer and seller interacted in some way,

00:06:13.090 --> 00:06:16.090
and an NFT is a receipt
of that transaction.

00:06:16.090 --> 00:06:19.110
That receipt often links
to something off-chain,

00:06:19.110 --> 00:06:21.190
often that is a JPEG.

00:06:21.190 --> 00:06:23.900
But the receipt itself
doesn't really tell you

00:06:23.900 --> 00:06:25.290
what the transaction was.

00:06:25.290 --> 00:06:27.230
To know what happened in that transaction,

00:06:27.230 --> 00:06:30.190
you have to look at the
terms of the original sale,

00:06:30.190 --> 00:06:31.023
the contract.

00:06:31.023 --> 00:06:33.440
You also have to look
at the reference work,

00:06:33.440 --> 00:06:35.270
often that's a JPEG.

00:06:35.270 --> 00:06:39.900
But the NFT itself is simply
a entry in a digital ledger,

00:06:39.900 --> 00:06:42.190
that's kind of like a
receipt in the real world.

00:06:42.190 --> 00:06:43.210
Okay, so with that understanding,

00:06:43.210 --> 00:06:45.750
I think we can get to the first
category of legal problems

00:06:45.750 --> 00:06:47.140
associated with NFTs,

00:06:47.140 --> 00:06:50.010
which are the problems
that are inherent on chain.

00:06:50.010 --> 00:06:52.760
And for that, I'm afraid
we're going to have to talk

00:06:52.760 --> 00:06:55.300
about fundamental contract theory.

00:06:55.300 --> 00:06:56.680
Honestly, I didn't think that this video

00:06:56.680 --> 00:06:57.590
was going to require me

00:06:57.590 --> 00:07:00.190
to get back to first
principles of contract law,

00:07:00.190 --> 00:07:01.410
the kind of thing that you learned

00:07:01.410 --> 00:07:05.110
in your first year of law
school, and yet here we are.

00:07:05.110 --> 00:07:07.210
And we need to understand
how a contract works,

00:07:07.210 --> 00:07:08.830
how it binds the parties

00:07:08.830 --> 00:07:11.190
and what the limitations of contracts are.

00:07:11.190 --> 00:07:12.820
And I know that sounds super boring,

00:07:12.820 --> 00:07:14.200
but I'm going to explain that

00:07:14.200 --> 00:07:17.100
with wrestler and
Renaissance man, John Cena,

00:07:17.100 --> 00:07:18.190
because in 2017,

00:07:18.190 --> 00:07:20.740
John Cena contracted with
the Ford Motor Company

00:07:20.740 --> 00:07:23.030
to buy the new Ford GT,

00:07:23.030 --> 00:07:25.440
the new hottest sports car at the time.

00:07:25.440 --> 00:07:27.530
In order for him to buy that car,

00:07:27.530 --> 00:07:29.510
he formed a contract with Ford.

00:07:29.510 --> 00:07:32.520
He offered to pay them hundreds
of thousands of dollars

00:07:32.520 --> 00:07:36.390
and Ford offered to give him a Ford GT.

00:07:36.390 --> 00:07:37.223
But in particular,

00:07:37.223 --> 00:07:40.550
there was one term that Ford
included in the sales contract,

00:07:40.550 --> 00:07:43.520
John Cena had to agree
not to sell his Ford GT

00:07:43.520 --> 00:07:44.920
for two years.

00:07:44.920 --> 00:07:47.430
And that as a perfectly
enforceable sale contract.

00:07:47.430 --> 00:07:49.750
Ford doesn't have to sell that to anyone.

00:07:49.750 --> 00:07:52.010
And so to make sure that
John Cena didn't turn around,

00:07:52.010 --> 00:07:54.450
flip the car for hundreds
of thousands of dollars,

00:07:54.450 --> 00:07:57.300
they made him promise not to
sell the car for two years.

00:07:57.300 --> 00:07:58.590
What did John Cena do?

00:07:58.590 --> 00:08:01.160
He turned her around and flipped
the car almost immediately

00:08:01.160 --> 00:08:03.120
and made hundreds of thousands of dollars.

00:08:03.120 --> 00:08:05.720
So what could Ford Motor Company do?

00:08:05.720 --> 00:08:07.130
Ford could sue John Cena

00:08:07.130 --> 00:08:08.750
for hundreds of thousands of dollars.

00:08:08.750 --> 00:08:10.610
And in fact, they did sue him

00:08:10.610 --> 00:08:12.840
and they settled for
an undisclosed amount.

00:08:12.840 --> 00:08:14.530
But the really interesting question is,

00:08:14.530 --> 00:08:16.320
what can Ford do against the person

00:08:16.320 --> 00:08:18.550
that bought the car from John Cena?

00:08:18.550 --> 00:08:21.710
And the answer is absolutely nothing

00:08:21.710 --> 00:08:24.230
because there's no contract between Ford

00:08:24.230 --> 00:08:27.230
and the person that bought
the car from John Cena.

00:08:27.230 --> 00:08:32.230
The secondary buyer is said to
not be in privity with Ford.

00:08:32.610 --> 00:08:34.210
John Cena and Ford are in privity,

00:08:34.210 --> 00:08:35.710
they have an actual contract,

00:08:35.710 --> 00:08:37.370
but there's nothing linking Ford

00:08:37.370 --> 00:08:39.080
and the downstream purchaser.

00:08:39.080 --> 00:08:43.220
So what in the world does
this have to do with NFTs?

00:08:43.220 --> 00:08:44.230
Well here's the thing,

00:08:44.230 --> 00:08:45.440
we talked about how an NFT

00:08:45.440 --> 00:08:48.400
doesn't really have any
attributes in and of itself.

00:08:48.400 --> 00:08:49.850
When you're buying an NFT,

00:08:49.850 --> 00:08:51.170
the real thing that you're buying

00:08:51.170 --> 00:08:54.950
is bound by the contract between
the buyer and the seller,

00:08:54.950 --> 00:08:57.780
those are the primary
purchaser and seller.

00:08:57.780 --> 00:09:00.200
There are some times when
there is no contract,

00:09:00.200 --> 00:09:01.080
we'll talk about that.

00:09:01.080 --> 00:09:02.530
But theoretically at least,

00:09:02.530 --> 00:09:06.310
the value of the NFT is
decided by the contract terms

00:09:06.310 --> 00:09:08.360
between the buyer and
the seller of the NFT.

00:09:08.360 --> 00:09:10.320
But one of the huge fundamental problems

00:09:10.320 --> 00:09:12.260
that we'll talk about a lot in a second,

00:09:12.260 --> 00:09:13.360
is that in practice,

00:09:13.360 --> 00:09:14.730
there's rarely any contract

00:09:14.730 --> 00:09:17.870
between the initial primary
buyer and the secondary buyers,

00:09:17.870 --> 00:09:20.370
and then all of the
downstream buyers later on.

00:09:20.370 --> 00:09:21.390
And on top of that,

00:09:21.390 --> 00:09:23.340
there is zero contractual privity

00:09:23.340 --> 00:09:27.650
between secondary buyers and
the original issuer of the NFT.

00:09:27.650 --> 00:09:28.760
That's a real problem

00:09:28.760 --> 00:09:30.900
when the only value associated with an NFT

00:09:30.900 --> 00:09:33.170
comes from the initial sale terms.

00:09:33.170 --> 00:09:35.600
So let's talk about an
example of this in practice.

00:09:35.600 --> 00:09:38.040
Many people are familiar
with NBA Top Shots,

00:09:38.040 --> 00:09:40.480
which was designed by Dapper Labs.

00:09:40.480 --> 00:09:42.730
NBA Top Shots is an NBA platform

00:09:42.730 --> 00:09:45.773
that purports to allow you to own moments

00:09:45.773 --> 00:09:48.070
that happen in the NBA,

00:09:48.070 --> 00:09:52.500
often small snippets of
videos related to NBA plays.

00:09:52.500 --> 00:09:55.010
Now, when you buy an NBA Top Shot NFT,

00:09:55.010 --> 00:09:57.030
the transaction's registered
on the flow blockchain,

00:09:57.030 --> 00:09:59.370
which was basically
created out of whole cloth

00:09:59.370 --> 00:10:01.980
by Dapper Labs to
facilitate the transactions

00:10:01.980 --> 00:10:03.810
of NBA Top Shots.

00:10:03.810 --> 00:10:06.010
When you initially buy an NBA Top Shot,

00:10:06.010 --> 00:10:10.000
you get limited rights to use
and view the NBA Top Shot,

00:10:10.000 --> 00:10:13.100
only within the NBA Top Shots platform.

00:10:13.100 --> 00:10:13.933
But here's the thing.

00:10:13.933 --> 00:10:16.580
NBA Top Shots is a private marketplace.

00:10:16.580 --> 00:10:19.080
The NFTs only have any real value

00:10:19.080 --> 00:10:22.110
when you use the walled
garden of NBA Top Shots,

00:10:22.110 --> 00:10:24.840
you have to use their app or their website

00:10:24.840 --> 00:10:27.840
for the NFT to have any
functionality at all.

00:10:27.840 --> 00:10:28.980
But in a way it works

00:10:28.980 --> 00:10:31.340
because everyone is on the same platform.

00:10:31.340 --> 00:10:36.340
Effectively NBA Top Shots is
simply a normal Web 2.0 website

00:10:36.440 --> 00:10:38.460
where you're allowed
to buy and sell things

00:10:38.460 --> 00:10:40.930
only within their specific platform.

00:10:40.930 --> 00:10:44.360
And the terms of sale travel
with the particular NFTs

00:10:44.360 --> 00:10:47.520
because everyone is buying
into the same system.

00:10:47.520 --> 00:10:50.950
If you somehow got an NBA Top Shot NFT

00:10:50.950 --> 00:10:54.070
and was able to sell it
on a different blockchain,

00:10:54.070 --> 00:10:56.660
well, odds are the terms
and conditions don't flow

00:10:56.660 --> 00:10:59.350
and you have an NFT that
has zero value at all,

00:10:59.350 --> 00:11:00.780
because it won't even work

00:11:00.780 --> 00:11:03.490
with the original NBA Top Shots platform.

00:11:03.490 --> 00:11:05.600
So if you are not NBA Top Shots

00:11:05.600 --> 00:11:07.150
and you're not forcing everyone

00:11:07.150 --> 00:11:09.660
to use the same walled garden,

00:11:09.660 --> 00:11:12.030
how do you buy the secondary purchasers?

00:11:12.030 --> 00:11:13.930
How do you even know what you're buying

00:11:13.930 --> 00:11:15.460
when you're buying an NFT?

00:11:15.460 --> 00:11:18.090
Well, that is incredibly controversial.

00:11:18.090 --> 00:11:20.620
Now, another famous example
is the Bored Ape Yacht Club

00:11:20.620 --> 00:11:25.280
that is famous for selling
NFTs of monkey cartoons.

00:11:25.280 --> 00:11:27.960
Now, you can buy a Bored
Ape NFT on OpenSea,

00:11:27.960 --> 00:11:30.290
which is a large marketplace for NFTs.

00:11:30.290 --> 00:11:31.350
When you do,

00:11:31.350 --> 00:11:32.670
the Bored Ape Yacht Club

00:11:32.670 --> 00:11:34.890
says that you get access to certain perks.

00:11:34.890 --> 00:11:37.650
These include getting access
to their discord server

00:11:37.650 --> 00:11:40.890
of like-minded purchasers of Bored Apes.

00:11:40.890 --> 00:11:41.740
But for the most part,

00:11:41.740 --> 00:11:43.920
those are perks that are offered off-chain

00:11:43.920 --> 00:11:46.580
and by the originator of the NFTs.

00:11:46.580 --> 00:11:48.660
Bored Ape could change their mind

00:11:48.660 --> 00:11:51.240
because it's questionable
whether the terms and conditions

00:11:51.240 --> 00:11:53.280
travel with the downstream purchasers

00:11:53.280 --> 00:11:55.350
of every single Bored Ape.

00:11:55.350 --> 00:11:57.250
If those terms and
conditions legally fail,

00:11:57.250 --> 00:11:59.180
say Bored Ape says they're not in privity

00:11:59.180 --> 00:12:01.200
with all of those downstream purchasers,

00:12:01.200 --> 00:12:03.770
and then they decide to cut off access

00:12:03.770 --> 00:12:05.510
to maybe some bad actors

00:12:05.510 --> 00:12:08.900
who were spamming the Bored Ape discord.

00:12:08.900 --> 00:12:10.560
Well, then those purchasers

00:12:10.560 --> 00:12:12.960
who spent hundreds of
thousands of dollars,

00:12:12.960 --> 00:12:14.640
they have zero recourse,

00:12:14.640 --> 00:12:17.370
because if they're not
in contractual privity,

00:12:17.370 --> 00:12:19.190
there's nothing that binds Bored Ape

00:12:19.190 --> 00:12:20.910
to provide those things long term.

00:12:20.910 --> 00:12:23.170
They may in fact provide those things,

00:12:23.170 --> 00:12:25.810
but they might not legally be required to.

00:12:25.810 --> 00:12:27.030
Now, if you're a normal website,

00:12:27.030 --> 00:12:30.070
you'd probably provide what's
called a click wrap agreement.

00:12:30.070 --> 00:12:31.740
That's one of those contracts

00:12:31.740 --> 00:12:34.360
that you scroll through
and then click I agree.

00:12:34.360 --> 00:12:37.490
Well, that provides a whole
bunch of terms and conditions.

00:12:37.490 --> 00:12:39.590
Sometimes those bind the users,

00:12:39.590 --> 00:12:42.940
sometimes that binds the platform itself.

00:12:42.940 --> 00:12:44.290
Now, when it comes to NFTs,

00:12:44.290 --> 00:12:46.510
you could provide a click wrap agreement,

00:12:46.510 --> 00:12:49.000
I don't see that being done very much.

00:12:49.000 --> 00:12:50.530
But there's a fundamental problem,

00:12:50.530 --> 00:12:54.300
the agreement isn't necessarily
linked to the NFT itself.

00:12:54.300 --> 00:12:57.300
You could also theoretically
put the terms of sale

00:12:57.300 --> 00:13:00.070
in the reference work
that's held off-chain.

00:13:00.070 --> 00:13:00.903
But again,

00:13:00.903 --> 00:13:03.110
that is something that is not necessarily

00:13:03.110 --> 00:13:06.820
connected to the NFT itself,
it can change over time.

00:13:06.820 --> 00:13:09.350
And you still have no
guarantee that the buyer

00:13:09.350 --> 00:13:12.040
has read the terms when
the sale is consummated.

00:13:12.040 --> 00:13:14.050
And as you have more and
more secondary purchasers

00:13:14.050 --> 00:13:17.120
and you get further and further
away from the initial sale,

00:13:17.120 --> 00:13:18.770
the problems just get harder and harder

00:13:18.770 --> 00:13:22.480
to make sure that all of
the downstream purchasers

00:13:22.480 --> 00:13:25.730
saw and assented to all
of the contract terms

00:13:25.730 --> 00:13:28.830
and to make sure that the prior sellers

00:13:28.830 --> 00:13:32.170
and the original minter
are bound by the same terms

00:13:32.170 --> 00:13:35.540
to provide the thing
that was origin provided

00:13:35.540 --> 00:13:37.220
when the NFT was minted.

00:13:37.220 --> 00:13:39.150
And the fundamental question,

00:13:39.150 --> 00:13:40.250
if you buy an NFT

00:13:40.250 --> 00:13:42.900
that doesn't have any
sale terms whatsoever,

00:13:42.900 --> 00:13:44.460
what have you purchased?

00:13:44.460 --> 00:13:46.870
The answer is probably nothing.

00:13:46.870 --> 00:13:49.320
At least nothing that the law can protect.

00:13:49.320 --> 00:13:50.170
We'll talk about this later,

00:13:50.170 --> 00:13:52.260
but one of the off-chain
problems with NFTs

00:13:52.260 --> 00:13:54.300
is that the reference work can change,

00:13:54.300 --> 00:13:55.830
you can get rug pulled.

00:13:55.830 --> 00:13:58.240
And if you don't have a contract

00:13:58.240 --> 00:14:01.050
that says you are entitled
to one specific thing,

00:14:01.050 --> 00:14:03.560
well, the NFT doesn't tell
you what the thing was

00:14:03.560 --> 00:14:05.130
that you were supposed to buy.

00:14:05.130 --> 00:14:07.690
In a way, an NFT is like a hyperlink

00:14:07.690 --> 00:14:10.490
and you can't own a hyperlink.

00:14:10.490 --> 00:14:13.280
So if you buy an NFT that
doesn't have contract terms,

00:14:13.280 --> 00:14:15.680
it doesn't define what you have purchased

00:14:15.680 --> 00:14:18.800
and you aren't familiar with
the terms of conditions,

00:14:18.800 --> 00:14:20.720
and you want to try and enforce that NFT

00:14:20.720 --> 00:14:23.300
against someone that
you purchased it from,

00:14:23.300 --> 00:14:26.130
I'm afraid to tell you that
I think you own about as much

00:14:26.130 --> 00:14:27.510
as a hyperlink,

00:14:27.510 --> 00:14:30.050
which is to say you can't own that.

00:14:30.050 --> 00:14:34.230
Many people claim that NFTs
supplant copyright law entirely.

00:14:34.230 --> 00:14:37.190
And unfortunately that's not even close.

00:14:37.190 --> 00:14:39.820
NFTs are a product of contract law,

00:14:39.820 --> 00:14:43.440
extremely fundamental
ideas of contract law.

00:14:43.440 --> 00:14:44.640
And there are real problems

00:14:44.640 --> 00:14:46.790
when it comes to enforcing those contracts

00:14:46.790 --> 00:14:47.850
and creating privity

00:14:47.850 --> 00:14:51.030
between the original minter
and downstream purchasers.

00:14:51.030 --> 00:14:52.640
Now, one of the things that NFTs can do,

00:14:52.640 --> 00:14:55.310
is that they can be attributed
with some basic programming,

00:14:55.310 --> 00:14:56.840
so called smart contracts.

00:14:56.840 --> 00:14:59.470
Now, these smart contracts
are basically contracts

00:14:59.470 --> 00:15:02.640
in the same sense that a
microchip is a contract,

00:15:02.640 --> 00:15:04.880
it's not a legally binding document,

00:15:04.880 --> 00:15:07.620
it just has a bit of
programming to do things

00:15:07.620 --> 00:15:08.800
when certain conditions are met.

00:15:08.800 --> 00:15:10.890
And these smart contracts seem to well

00:15:10.890 --> 00:15:13.930
when they are based on
internet native occurrences

00:15:13.930 --> 00:15:15.630
and simple transactions,

00:15:15.630 --> 00:15:17.260
the kind of thing like an escrow account

00:15:17.260 --> 00:15:19.180
where you don't release funds

00:15:19.180 --> 00:15:21.710
until certain conditions
precedent are met.

00:15:21.710 --> 00:15:23.040
But these smart contracts,

00:15:23.040 --> 00:15:25.620
these tiny programs
are not real contracts,

00:15:25.620 --> 00:15:28.230
they don't bind the downstream purchasers.

00:15:28.230 --> 00:15:30.650
And in fact, as we've seen,

00:15:30.650 --> 00:15:32.270
they can break over time.

00:15:32.270 --> 00:15:34.230
There are no legal rights that are created

00:15:34.230 --> 00:15:36.730
as a result of the smart
contracts themselves.

00:15:36.730 --> 00:15:39.760
So for better or worse,
lawyers exist for a reason,

00:15:39.760 --> 00:15:41.960
and programmers are really, really bad

00:15:41.960 --> 00:15:43.750
at planning for contingencies.

00:15:43.750 --> 00:15:45.260
And they're also really bad

00:15:45.260 --> 00:15:48.210
at understanding contract law in general.

00:15:48.210 --> 00:15:52.440
So the bottom line is that
NFTs are a product of contract,

00:15:52.440 --> 00:15:55.110
the terms under which you bought them.

00:15:55.110 --> 00:15:57.340
So for all of those who purchased in NFTs,

00:15:57.340 --> 00:15:59.490
I'm sure that you all checked
the terms and conditions

00:15:59.490 --> 00:16:01.180
before you purchased, right?

00:16:01.180 --> 00:16:04.390
You got terms and conditions, right?

00:16:04.390 --> 00:16:05.500
And that brings us to copyright

00:16:05.500 --> 00:16:08.180
because NFTs do not
supplant copyright law,

00:16:08.180 --> 00:16:09.120
not even close.

00:16:09.120 --> 00:16:13.530
NFTs are bound by normal
everyday rules of copyright.

00:16:13.530 --> 00:16:14.740
When you buy an NFT,

00:16:14.740 --> 00:16:16.080
sometimes you get the copyright,

00:16:16.080 --> 00:16:17.800
but most of the times you don't.

00:16:17.800 --> 00:16:19.590
Again, it entirely depends

00:16:19.590 --> 00:16:23.220
on the original terms of
sale related to the NFT.

00:16:23.220 --> 00:16:24.590
NBA Top shots for example,

00:16:24.590 --> 00:16:28.240
does not give you a copyright
in the original video

00:16:28.240 --> 00:16:29.240
to the NBA.

00:16:29.240 --> 00:16:31.920
They give you an extremely limited license

00:16:31.920 --> 00:16:35.730
to use that video in
extremely limited places

00:16:35.730 --> 00:16:36.760
and nothing more.

00:16:36.760 --> 00:16:37.740
Some other famous examples

00:16:37.740 --> 00:16:39.500
where people think they're
buying the copyright,

00:16:39.500 --> 00:16:42.970
but aren't include CryptoAds
and Bored Ape Yacht Club.

00:16:42.970 --> 00:16:44.560
With CryptoAds, they basically operate

00:16:44.560 --> 00:16:46.260
under the Creative Commons Zero License,

00:16:46.260 --> 00:16:49.030
where if you buy a CryptoAds JPEG,

00:16:49.030 --> 00:16:52.460
they allow you to do basically
whatever you want with them.

00:16:52.460 --> 00:16:56.240
But again, CryptoAds hangs
on to the original copyright.

00:16:56.240 --> 00:16:59.080
They simply license it
to downstream purchasers.

00:16:59.080 --> 00:17:01.930
Same thing with the Bored Ape Yacht Club,

00:17:01.930 --> 00:17:04.800
they offer a fairly expansive license,

00:17:04.800 --> 00:17:07.900
but again, they hold the IP centrally.

00:17:07.900 --> 00:17:09.870
And you're just simply given a license.

00:17:09.870 --> 00:17:11.810
You are not buying the copyright

00:17:11.810 --> 00:17:16.530
associated with the Bored Ape
cartoon that you purchased.

00:17:16.530 --> 00:17:19.300
And with Bored Ape, the
license is held off-chain,

00:17:19.300 --> 00:17:22.410
which means that that license
might change over time

00:17:22.410 --> 00:17:24.380
and you might never even know about it.

00:17:24.380 --> 00:17:27.040
And often this has very little
to do with copyright at all.

00:17:27.040 --> 00:17:29.410
If you buy an NBA Top Shot,

00:17:29.410 --> 00:17:31.690
let's say of a particular clip

00:17:31.690 --> 00:17:34.200
of something that happened in the NBA,

00:17:34.200 --> 00:17:36.150
you can't enforce that against someone

00:17:36.150 --> 00:17:39.640
who is displaying that piece
of video somewhere else.

00:17:39.640 --> 00:17:40.700
And on top of that,

00:17:40.700 --> 00:17:43.870
the NBA can prevent you from displaying

00:17:43.870 --> 00:17:45.650
that particular video clip,

00:17:45.650 --> 00:17:47.120
if you're displaying it in a way

00:17:47.120 --> 00:17:48.830
that conflicts with the license

00:17:48.830 --> 00:17:51.500
that was purchased through NBA Top Shots,

00:17:51.500 --> 00:17:54.000
which again is incredibly restrictive.

00:17:54.000 --> 00:17:55.990
And the issue of copyright
is incredibly important

00:17:55.990 --> 00:17:58.070
because when you're
talking about digital art,

00:17:58.070 --> 00:18:02.210
that's often the only right
that the law recognizes.

00:18:02.210 --> 00:18:04.070
If you don't have the copyright,

00:18:04.070 --> 00:18:06.140
then you effectively have nothing.

00:18:06.140 --> 00:18:09.160
And that's important because
in a context of NFTs,

00:18:09.160 --> 00:18:12.240
a lot of the art that
is being bought and sold

00:18:12.240 --> 00:18:14.660
probably doesn't have a copyright at all,

00:18:14.660 --> 00:18:16.550
it's copyrightable.

00:18:16.550 --> 00:18:19.270
The ultimate irony is that
when it comes to digital art

00:18:19.270 --> 00:18:20.650
that does have a copyright,

00:18:20.650 --> 00:18:24.240
ironically, the copyright
is a scarce resource.

00:18:24.240 --> 00:18:25.550
There's only one.

00:18:25.550 --> 00:18:28.610
Sure you can license it an
infinite number of times,

00:18:28.610 --> 00:18:31.250
or you can assign the copyright,

00:18:31.250 --> 00:18:35.930
but the copyright itself
is inherently scarce.

00:18:35.930 --> 00:18:37.470
And maybe we never needed to solve

00:18:37.470 --> 00:18:39.370
the digital scarcity question at all,

00:18:39.370 --> 00:18:42.690
because it was already solved
through regular copyright law.

00:18:42.690 --> 00:18:44.080
Because a lot of the reference art

00:18:44.080 --> 00:18:47.200
that's associated with an
NFT is called generative art.

00:18:47.200 --> 00:18:48.980
It's art that's created by AI,

00:18:48.980 --> 00:18:51.440
or programmatically through a computer.

00:18:51.440 --> 00:18:53.819
You might have heard of
the Ninth Circuit Case,

00:18:53.819 --> 00:18:55.450
Naruto versus Slater.

00:18:55.450 --> 00:18:57.260
That is the monkey selfie case

00:18:57.260 --> 00:19:01.640
where the courts recognize that
only a human can create art

00:19:01.640 --> 00:19:02.900
that has a copyright.

00:19:02.900 --> 00:19:04.480
In that particular case,

00:19:04.480 --> 00:19:05.313
there was a monkey

00:19:05.313 --> 00:19:08.780
that got a hold of a photographer's
camera, took a selfie,

00:19:08.780 --> 00:19:11.640
and the photographer
tried exercise a copyright

00:19:11.640 --> 00:19:13.560
in that particular piece of artwork.

00:19:13.560 --> 00:19:14.560
At the same time,

00:19:14.560 --> 00:19:17.850
PETA said that the monkey
owned the copyright

00:19:17.850 --> 00:19:19.670
to that particular photo,

00:19:19.670 --> 00:19:22.460
and they intervened to try to
exercise the monkey's rights

00:19:22.460 --> 00:19:23.780
on its behalf.

00:19:23.780 --> 00:19:26.730
And the court found that
no one had a copyright

00:19:26.730 --> 00:19:29.960
because only humans can
have a copyright in art.

00:19:29.960 --> 00:19:33.180
Only humans can create
art that is copyrightable.

00:19:33.180 --> 00:19:34.140
So by extension,

00:19:34.140 --> 00:19:36.700
if a machine is deemed to
be the author of a work,

00:19:36.700 --> 00:19:41.160
no one can exercise a copyright
in that particular artwork.

00:19:41.160 --> 00:19:42.580
And in the context of NFTs,

00:19:42.580 --> 00:19:44.380
there's untold numbers of works

00:19:44.380 --> 00:19:47.170
that are touted as being
created by computers

00:19:47.170 --> 00:19:50.040
that's deemed to be a feature, not a bug.

00:19:50.040 --> 00:19:51.700
And if that's the case,

00:19:51.700 --> 00:19:54.450
I don't think that there is a
copyright there to begin with.

00:19:54.450 --> 00:19:58.050
No one can exercise any rights
associated with that artwork

00:19:58.050 --> 00:20:00.630
because no human created it.

00:20:00.630 --> 00:20:03.750
Even with a very famous
Bored Ape Yacht Club,

00:20:03.750 --> 00:20:05.440
it seems to be just a rehashing

00:20:05.440 --> 00:20:07.970
of the same elements over
and over and over again,

00:20:07.970 --> 00:20:11.010
which I believe were hashed by a computer

00:20:11.010 --> 00:20:12.030
rather than person.

00:20:12.030 --> 00:20:14.660
A person may have created
the individual elements

00:20:14.660 --> 00:20:15.860
once upon a time,

00:20:15.860 --> 00:20:19.080
but the individual artworks
were created by a computer.

00:20:19.080 --> 00:20:20.840
Now there are some complicated issues

00:20:20.840 --> 00:20:24.670
related to copyrights of
compilations and works for hire,

00:20:24.670 --> 00:20:25.890
but there is an argument

00:20:25.890 --> 00:20:29.010
that some very, very expensive NFTs

00:20:29.010 --> 00:20:32.340
simply don't have a copyright at all.

00:20:32.340 --> 00:20:35.220
Now in fairness to
NFTs, in the real world,

00:20:35.220 --> 00:20:39.090
when you buy real art, you
buy a physical painting,

00:20:39.090 --> 00:20:41.370
it's normal not to buy the copyright

00:20:41.370 --> 00:20:43.490
associated with that particular painting.

00:20:43.490 --> 00:20:45.260
When you buy a novel,

00:20:45.260 --> 00:20:49.190
you are not buying the copyright
associated with that novel.

00:20:49.190 --> 00:20:51.500
So it's not unusual in the art world

00:20:51.500 --> 00:20:55.160
to not get the copyright
associated with a work.

00:20:55.160 --> 00:20:56.800
And so, in some sense,

00:20:56.800 --> 00:20:59.010
it's normal not to get a copyright

00:20:59.010 --> 00:21:01.290
associated with digital art.

00:21:01.290 --> 00:21:03.500
The issue here is that digital art

00:21:03.500 --> 00:21:06.620
doesn't have a physical token.

00:21:06.620 --> 00:21:08.260
If you aren't getting

00:21:08.260 --> 00:21:10.410
the copyright associated
with the digital art,

00:21:10.410 --> 00:21:12.130
you're not really getting anything

00:21:12.130 --> 00:21:14.380
that is protectable under the law.

00:21:14.380 --> 00:21:15.690
But of course, in the real world,

00:21:15.690 --> 00:21:18.410
you actually have a physical thing.

00:21:18.410 --> 00:21:20.260
And that's associated
with a legal doctrine

00:21:20.260 --> 00:21:21.720
called the First Sale Doctrine

00:21:21.720 --> 00:21:23.670
that when you buy a physical good,

00:21:23.670 --> 00:21:26.280
whether it is a painting
or a pair of Nike shoes,

00:21:26.280 --> 00:21:28.700
you can basically do whatever you want

00:21:28.700 --> 00:21:29.940
to that physical good.

00:21:29.940 --> 00:21:32.380
You don't have necessarily
the right to reproduce

00:21:32.380 --> 00:21:34.220
or otherwise use an image

00:21:34.220 --> 00:21:35.800
of the thing that you have purchased,

00:21:35.800 --> 00:21:36.633
but you do have the right

00:21:36.633 --> 00:21:38.550
to basically do whatever you want

00:21:38.550 --> 00:21:39.730
with the physical good itself,

00:21:39.730 --> 00:21:42.320
including selling it to somebody else.

00:21:42.320 --> 00:21:45.490
Physical works of art are almost
inherently scarce as well.

00:21:45.490 --> 00:21:49.040
A painting can only be
painted by a human once.

00:21:49.040 --> 00:21:51.490
And NFTs are touted as solving

00:21:51.490 --> 00:21:55.050
the infinite reproducibility
problem with digital works.

00:21:55.050 --> 00:21:56.820
Where legally speaking at least,

00:21:56.820 --> 00:22:00.210
no NFT can claim to have
Providence over an artwork

00:22:00.210 --> 00:22:03.740
that is itself legally
infinitely reproducible.

00:22:03.740 --> 00:22:06.450
So when it comes to
digital art, such as JPEGs,

00:22:06.450 --> 00:22:08.450
if you're not buying the copyright,

00:22:08.450 --> 00:22:11.170
I'm not sure legally what you are buying.

00:22:11.170 --> 00:22:12.920
And as a non-legal aside,

00:22:12.920 --> 00:22:14.720
I know art is inherently subjective

00:22:14.720 --> 00:22:16.780
and there's no objectivity in art

00:22:16.780 --> 00:22:19.550
and claiming that something
is good art or bad art

00:22:19.550 --> 00:22:21.270
is inherently inchoate,

00:22:21.270 --> 00:22:24.570
but I mean, just look at
this stuff, this is bad art.

00:22:24.570 --> 00:22:25.920
And if you're claiming that this art

00:22:25.920 --> 00:22:28.080
is inherently worth hundreds
of thousands of dollars,

00:22:28.080 --> 00:22:30.053
apart from an any speculative value,

00:22:30.890 --> 00:22:32.620
I don't think you're good at art.

00:22:32.620 --> 00:22:35.370
So that takes us to
consumer protection issues.

00:22:35.370 --> 00:22:37.230
We've seen that there is a vast Gulf

00:22:37.230 --> 00:22:39.470
between what people think they are getting

00:22:39.470 --> 00:22:43.200
and what they're actually
getting when it comes to NFTs.

00:22:43.200 --> 00:22:45.870
And these NFTs are widely promoted

00:22:45.870 --> 00:22:47.870
by a wide variety of different people,

00:22:47.870 --> 00:22:50.630
including the people that
are selling these things

00:22:50.630 --> 00:22:53.220
and people who are paid to promote them.

00:22:53.220 --> 00:22:55.160
And it doesn't seem
that anyone really knows

00:22:55.160 --> 00:22:57.410
exactly what they are receiving.

00:22:57.410 --> 00:22:59.480
Platforms like NBA Top Shots,

00:22:59.480 --> 00:23:03.170
who you can assume have
legions of lawyers,

00:23:03.170 --> 00:23:05.190
have actually changed a lot
of the marketing language

00:23:05.190 --> 00:23:06.400
that they've used.

00:23:06.400 --> 00:23:09.520
Historically they used
language like owning a moment,

00:23:09.520 --> 00:23:11.500
but that probably doesn't line up

00:23:11.500 --> 00:23:15.200
with the idea of getting an
extremely limited IP license

00:23:15.200 --> 00:23:17.330
to view and display the reference work,

00:23:17.330 --> 00:23:20.490
almost exclusively on
the Top Shots platform.

00:23:20.490 --> 00:23:23.360
Now they use language like scoring in NFT,

00:23:23.360 --> 00:23:25.220
or collecting in NFT,

00:23:25.220 --> 00:23:27.300
which to me, is very, very different

00:23:27.300 --> 00:23:29.280
than the idea of owning a moment.

00:23:29.280 --> 00:23:32.550
And it almost goes without
saying, but I'll say it anyway,

00:23:32.550 --> 00:23:34.510
you can't own a moment,

00:23:34.510 --> 00:23:37.150
that is not a thing
that the law recognizes

00:23:37.150 --> 00:23:38.670
that anyone can own.

00:23:38.670 --> 00:23:42.880
You might as well try and buy
a star in a far away galaxy,

00:23:42.880 --> 00:23:44.420
you can't own that.

00:23:44.420 --> 00:23:47.360
And it seems like there is a
huge amount of misperception

00:23:47.360 --> 00:23:49.110
in terms of what people
think they're getting

00:23:49.110 --> 00:23:50.180
in terms of ownership.

00:23:50.180 --> 00:23:52.150
And of course the false promises

00:23:52.150 --> 00:23:54.600
from the minters are
compounding that effect.

00:23:54.600 --> 00:23:57.590
And whenever you have
false promises from sellers

00:23:57.590 --> 00:23:59.540
and misperceptions of buyers,

00:23:59.540 --> 00:24:02.560
you run into consumer protection issues.

00:24:02.560 --> 00:24:06.040
If I was a minter or a seller of NFTs,

00:24:06.040 --> 00:24:08.830
I'd make darn sure that
every single person

00:24:08.830 --> 00:24:10.530
saw the terms and conditions,

00:24:10.530 --> 00:24:13.870
so that there was no confusion
as to what I was selling

00:24:13.870 --> 00:24:15.600
and what they were buying,

00:24:15.600 --> 00:24:18.720
but that's just simply not
happening in this industry.

00:24:18.720 --> 00:24:21.030
And while many people would
probably try and claim

00:24:21.030 --> 00:24:22.650
first amendment protection,

00:24:22.650 --> 00:24:25.940
the first amendment does
not extend nearly as much

00:24:25.940 --> 00:24:28.810
to buyers and sellers of goods.

00:24:28.810 --> 00:24:32.250
When you have commercial
sales and commercial speech,

00:24:32.250 --> 00:24:34.160
the first amendment doesn't
protect nearly as much

00:24:34.160 --> 00:24:35.470
as you'd think it would.

00:24:35.470 --> 00:24:36.303
And as we discussed,

00:24:36.303 --> 00:24:39.260
many NFTs drop without
any terms whatsoever.

00:24:39.260 --> 00:24:40.980
And for those that do have terms,

00:24:40.980 --> 00:24:41.830
it's not at all clear

00:24:41.830 --> 00:24:44.610
that those terms travel
with downstream purchasers

00:24:44.610 --> 00:24:46.089
of those NFTs.

00:24:46.089 --> 00:24:48.540
NFTs are also a product of programming.

00:24:48.540 --> 00:24:50.420
And as we've seen time and time again,

00:24:50.420 --> 00:24:52.700
those programs can fail.

00:24:52.700 --> 00:24:54.530
An NFT can be corrupted,

00:24:54.530 --> 00:24:56.620
the reference work can be rug pulled,

00:24:56.620 --> 00:24:58.420
you can change the reference work,

00:24:58.420 --> 00:25:01.930
even if you're pointing to an IPFS file,

00:25:01.930 --> 00:25:03.810
that can change.

00:25:03.810 --> 00:25:06.990
Who's responsible if the
NFT isn't even delivering

00:25:06.990 --> 00:25:09.170
the things that it was
supposed to deliver,

00:25:09.170 --> 00:25:11.070
if those terms exist?

00:25:11.070 --> 00:25:14.120
Often NFTs provide the
division of royalties

00:25:14.120 --> 00:25:15.850
and revenue streams,

00:25:15.850 --> 00:25:19.870
but those often only work if
the blockchain is working.

00:25:19.870 --> 00:25:22.270
You better hope that the
blockchain doesn't fork,

00:25:22.270 --> 00:25:23.610
that the programming works

00:25:23.610 --> 00:25:25.650
and that it provides for
every single contingency

00:25:25.650 --> 00:25:26.483
that you can think of,

00:25:26.483 --> 00:25:29.420
because those things break all the time.

00:25:29.420 --> 00:25:30.740
And there are all kinds of laws

00:25:30.740 --> 00:25:33.270
that make sure that
consumers are protected

00:25:33.270 --> 00:25:35.280
by contracts of adhesion.

00:25:35.280 --> 00:25:36.810
There is the ESIGN act

00:25:36.810 --> 00:25:40.390
that governs whether a click
wrap agreement on a website

00:25:40.390 --> 00:25:45.390
is enforceable, both against
the seller and to the buyer.

00:25:45.440 --> 00:25:48.210
These are effectively the
se same kinds of issues

00:25:48.210 --> 00:25:49.930
that any kind of eCommerce website

00:25:49.930 --> 00:25:51.470
is going to have to deal with.

00:25:51.470 --> 00:25:55.530
But effectively every single
individual buyer and seller

00:25:55.530 --> 00:25:58.350
of an NFT have to deal
with the same thing,

00:25:58.350 --> 00:26:00.990
and many people just aren't.

00:26:00.990 --> 00:26:03.490
Making sure that contract
terms are enforceable

00:26:03.490 --> 00:26:06.300
is a known issue that all websites,

00:26:06.300 --> 00:26:08.670
whether it's Web 2.0 or Web 3,

00:26:08.670 --> 00:26:09.540
have to deal with.

00:26:09.540 --> 00:26:12.920
And you might try and put
a contract on OpenSea,

00:26:12.920 --> 00:26:14.100
if that is the marketplace

00:26:14.100 --> 00:26:15.940
that you are trying to sell your NFT,

00:26:15.940 --> 00:26:17.880
but right now there's no guarantee

00:26:17.880 --> 00:26:19.190
that anyone's going to read it.

00:26:19.190 --> 00:26:21.830
And if no one's reading
the contract terms,

00:26:21.830 --> 00:26:24.410
you've got big issues in
terms of enforceability,

00:26:24.410 --> 00:26:25.243
let alone privity.

00:26:25.243 --> 00:26:27.450
And the bottom line is
that you have to make sure

00:26:27.450 --> 00:26:30.800
that consumer are affirmatively consenting

00:26:30.800 --> 00:26:32.300
to the contract terms.

00:26:32.300 --> 00:26:36.340
And if you can't prove that
that might be your problem,

00:26:36.340 --> 00:26:39.980
but it also might be the
problem of the NFT purchasers.

00:26:39.980 --> 00:26:42.010
If they wanna make sure that third parties

00:26:42.010 --> 00:26:45.730
are providing off-chain unlocked goods,

00:26:45.730 --> 00:26:48.170
well, you'd better have a
contract that makes sure

00:26:48.170 --> 00:26:50.680
that they actually provide those things.

00:26:50.680 --> 00:26:51.750
And then there's all kinds

00:26:51.750 --> 00:26:53.820
of securities related legal issues.

00:26:53.820 --> 00:26:57.070
NFTs are being market mostly
as art or collectibles,

00:26:57.070 --> 00:26:58.200
but at the same time,

00:26:58.200 --> 00:27:01.050
there is so much hype
and so much speculation,

00:27:01.050 --> 00:27:04.410
people tout them at as incredible
investment opportunities,

00:27:04.410 --> 00:27:05.250
premised on the idea

00:27:05.250 --> 00:27:08.360
that they will always increase in value.

00:27:08.360 --> 00:27:10.100
At the moment it's incredibly unclear,

00:27:10.100 --> 00:27:12.400
but it's possible that the
Securities Exchange Commission,

00:27:12.400 --> 00:27:16.000
the SEC, could classify
certain NFTs as security.

00:27:16.000 --> 00:27:18.540
If they are classified as securities,

00:27:18.540 --> 00:27:20.750
this triggers all kinds
of regulatory issues

00:27:20.750 --> 00:27:22.540
with potential liabilities.

00:27:22.540 --> 00:27:24.170
And while it currently unclear,

00:27:24.170 --> 00:27:26.340
courts will use what's
called the Howey Test

00:27:26.340 --> 00:27:29.510
to determine whether these
things are securities or not.

00:27:29.510 --> 00:27:31.880
That stems from a Supreme Court case.

00:27:31.880 --> 00:27:32.910
Under this test,

00:27:32.910 --> 00:27:36.097
a transaction is deemed an
investment contract if a person,

00:27:36.097 --> 00:27:38.207
"Invest his money in a common enterprise

00:27:38.207 --> 00:27:41.467
"and is led to expect profits
solely from the efforts

00:27:41.467 --> 00:27:44.020
"of the promoter or a third party."

00:27:44.020 --> 00:27:46.920
This was a huge problem for the ICO,

00:27:46.920 --> 00:27:49.880
the Initial Coin Offering boom of 2017,

00:27:49.880 --> 00:27:51.760
because those Initial Coin Offerings

00:27:51.760 --> 00:27:53.560
ran a foul of the Howey Test

00:27:53.560 --> 00:27:55.960
and were regulated as securities.

00:27:55.960 --> 00:28:00.170
Effectively, people offered
coins on a blockchain,

00:28:00.170 --> 00:28:02.250
related to some enterprise,

00:28:02.250 --> 00:28:05.080
for a thing that had not
yet been and created.

00:28:05.080 --> 00:28:05.913
And as a result,

00:28:05.913 --> 00:28:08.210
you rarely see Initial
Coin Offerings anymore,

00:28:08.210 --> 00:28:09.940
at least not from companies

00:28:09.940 --> 00:28:12.940
that are trying to abide by the law.

00:28:12.940 --> 00:28:14.840
Now, the argument against NFTs securities

00:28:14.840 --> 00:28:16.430
is that NFTs are finished products,

00:28:16.430 --> 00:28:19.000
not investment vehicles
that need to be managed.

00:28:19.000 --> 00:28:20.610
If NFTs appreciate in value,

00:28:20.610 --> 00:28:22.560
it's through principles like scarcity

00:28:22.560 --> 00:28:24.370
and just market forces,

00:28:24.370 --> 00:28:25.810
not because of a third party's effort

00:28:25.810 --> 00:28:27.720
to enhance the NFTs value.

00:28:27.720 --> 00:28:30.567
And the SEC's 2019 framework says,

00:28:30.567 --> 00:28:31.737
"Price appreciation

00:28:31.737 --> 00:28:34.137
"resulting solely from
external market forces,

00:28:34.137 --> 00:28:37.017
"such as general inflationary
trends or the economy,

00:28:37.017 --> 00:28:39.907
"impacting the supply and
demand of an underlying asset,

00:28:39.907 --> 00:28:42.900
"generally is not considered
profit under Howey Test."

00:28:42.900 --> 00:28:44.360
So an increase in value alone

00:28:44.360 --> 00:28:46.750
doesn't make something a security.

00:28:46.750 --> 00:28:48.590
However, some of the proposed uses of NFTs

00:28:48.590 --> 00:28:49.640
are a much closer call

00:28:49.640 --> 00:28:51.810
and would probably be
considered securities

00:28:51.810 --> 00:28:53.130
if push came to shove.

00:28:53.130 --> 00:28:55.310
Things like royalty payments to artists,

00:28:55.310 --> 00:28:58.430
especially if the art
hasn't been created yet,

00:28:58.430 --> 00:29:00.350
would be a much closer call.

00:29:00.350 --> 00:29:03.570
And even apart from
securities related regulation,

00:29:03.570 --> 00:29:06.317
there seems to be all kinds
of just garden variety fraud.

00:29:06.317 --> 00:29:11.317
The NFT markets are rife
with rug pulls and Rickrolls,

00:29:11.410 --> 00:29:14.190
people who are intentionally
scamming other people

00:29:14.190 --> 00:29:16.800
out of hundreds of thousands,
if not millions of dollars.

00:29:16.800 --> 00:29:19.300
Not in the sense that NFTs
in general are a scam,

00:29:19.300 --> 00:29:22.160
but literally these
things are actual scams

00:29:22.160 --> 00:29:24.850
where they're making false promises,

00:29:24.850 --> 00:29:26.220
knowing that they're false

00:29:26.220 --> 00:29:27.295
and then not providing

00:29:27.295 --> 00:29:29.830
on the thing that they promise to provide.

00:29:29.830 --> 00:29:32.770
I mean, that is just
good old fashioned fraud.

00:29:32.770 --> 00:29:34.160
But don't take my word for it,

00:29:34.160 --> 00:29:37.720
I turn to the foremost expert
in crypto related scams,

00:29:37.720 --> 00:29:41.720
Coffeezilla, to give me
a recent salient example.

00:29:41.720 --> 00:29:45.070
- One example of when
an NFT project promised

00:29:45.070 --> 00:29:47.670
and then didn't deliver
on what it said it would,

00:29:47.670 --> 00:29:50.130
was De'Aaron Fox's project,

00:29:50.130 --> 00:29:51.313
called SwipaTheFox.

00:29:52.470 --> 00:29:54.990
De'Aaron is an NBA player
for the Sacramento Kings

00:29:54.990 --> 00:29:59.480
and he recently pulled in
$1.5 million in NFT sales,

00:29:59.480 --> 00:30:02.250
in part due to a roadmap
where he had promised

00:30:02.250 --> 00:30:03.720
some pretty extraordinary things,

00:30:03.720 --> 00:30:06.890
which would give these NFTs
some real world utility.

00:30:06.890 --> 00:30:07.760
For example,

00:30:07.760 --> 00:30:10.910
one of the perks was gonna be
a Metaverse basketball court,

00:30:10.910 --> 00:30:14.560
five scholarships, charity
giveaways, free merch,

00:30:14.560 --> 00:30:16.720
even tickets to All Star games.

00:30:16.720 --> 00:30:18.620
These all gave investors the impression

00:30:18.620 --> 00:30:21.640
that these NFTs might
have real world value

00:30:21.640 --> 00:30:23.170
and be worth buying.

00:30:23.170 --> 00:30:25.260
However, right after the money came in,

00:30:25.260 --> 00:30:26.960
De'Aaron Fox issued a statement

00:30:26.960 --> 00:30:29.680
saying he was too busy with his NBA career

00:30:29.680 --> 00:30:32.350
to make all that happen and
was quitting the project.

00:30:32.350 --> 00:30:34.050
No refunds, of course.

00:30:34.050 --> 00:30:36.900
Apparently he wasn't too
busy to start the NFT,

00:30:36.900 --> 00:30:39.960
just too busy to deliver
on what he promised.

00:30:39.960 --> 00:30:42.890
- Then of course, there's all
kinds market manipulations.

00:30:42.890 --> 00:30:45.630
Many of the high dollar
amount transactions

00:30:45.630 --> 00:30:47.850
related to very high ticket NFTs,

00:30:47.850 --> 00:30:51.000
are not conducted by
arms length transactions.

00:30:51.000 --> 00:30:52.570
They might be wash sales,

00:30:52.570 --> 00:30:56.440
where an individual sells
an NFT to themselves,

00:30:56.440 --> 00:30:58.430
using a sock puppet,

00:30:58.430 --> 00:31:01.870
or there might be coordination
between two different people

00:31:01.870 --> 00:31:05.170
who agree to pay a high
price for something

00:31:05.170 --> 00:31:07.260
to prop up the price.

00:31:07.260 --> 00:31:10.130
Because the blockchain is quasi anonymous,

00:31:10.130 --> 00:31:12.080
pseudo anonymous if you
want to get technical,

00:31:12.080 --> 00:31:15.494
it's very easy for people to
create sock puppet accounts,

00:31:15.494 --> 00:31:17.360
that they control,

00:31:17.360 --> 00:31:19.290
so that they can show a price history

00:31:19.290 --> 00:31:22.680
of their NFT being sold
for lots and lots of money,

00:31:22.680 --> 00:31:25.830
so that some unsuspecting
downstream purchaser thinks,

00:31:25.830 --> 00:31:27.670
well, if that person paid this much,

00:31:27.670 --> 00:31:29.040
then I'll pay even more

00:31:29.040 --> 00:31:30.650
and I'll be able to sell it

00:31:30.650 --> 00:31:33.340
to somebody else down the
line for even more than that.

00:31:33.340 --> 00:31:36.960
Now, this to me raises garden
variety issues of fraud.

00:31:36.960 --> 00:31:40.490
It's just out and out fraud
and market manipulation.

00:31:40.490 --> 00:31:44.010
It could even be a violation
of the Sherman Antitrust Act.

00:31:44.010 --> 00:31:45.600
This is the kind of anti-competitive

00:31:45.600 --> 00:31:49.910
behavior that the DOJ and
FTC likes to crack down on.

00:31:49.910 --> 00:31:52.060
And often when you get
into price manipulation

00:31:52.060 --> 00:31:54.220
among several different people,

00:31:54.220 --> 00:31:56.820
well, then you're getting
into criminal liability

00:31:56.820 --> 00:31:58.880
under the Sherman Act.

00:31:58.880 --> 00:32:00.880
Maybe there's an infinite
supply of greater fools

00:32:00.880 --> 00:32:02.430
when the prices keep going up,

00:32:02.430 --> 00:32:04.800
but as soon as any particular NFT crashes

00:32:04.800 --> 00:32:08.090
and a buyer realizes that
there was price manipulation

00:32:08.090 --> 00:32:10.140
before they purchased their NFT,

00:32:10.140 --> 00:32:12.340
they might come looking
for some heads to roll,

00:32:12.340 --> 00:32:15.120
and there might be some real
civil and criminal liability

00:32:15.120 --> 00:32:16.080
associated with people

00:32:16.080 --> 00:32:18.610
who were propping up the prices unfairly.

00:32:18.610 --> 00:32:20.860
And then there's lots of
just pump and dump schemes.

00:32:20.860 --> 00:32:22.900
People who maybe aren't
promoting an out and out scam,

00:32:22.900 --> 00:32:25.320
but are looking to a quick buck.

00:32:25.320 --> 00:32:28.050
And there's right now
a proposed class action

00:32:28.050 --> 00:32:30.940
against people who promoted Ethereum Max,

00:32:30.940 --> 00:32:32.070
people like Kim Kardashian,

00:32:32.070 --> 00:32:34.200
Floyd Mayweather, Paul Pierce,

00:32:34.200 --> 00:32:37.700
they promoted this token on
their social media chains,

00:32:37.700 --> 00:32:39.610
and right now there's a class action

00:32:39.610 --> 00:32:42.717
because it was clearly
a pump and dump scheme.

00:32:42.717 --> 00:32:44.250
And the Ethereum Max lawsuit

00:32:44.250 --> 00:32:46.090
doesn't rely on federal securities law,

00:32:46.090 --> 00:32:48.910
it relies on pretty normal
state based causes of action,

00:32:48.910 --> 00:32:50.400
like unjust enrichment,

00:32:50.400 --> 00:32:53.060
violations of California's
Unfair Competition Law

00:32:53.060 --> 00:32:55.800
and the California Consumer
Legal Remedies Act.

00:32:55.800 --> 00:32:58.110
I don't know that we will
ever know for certain

00:32:58.110 --> 00:33:00.940
how many NFTs are sold in wash sales,

00:33:00.940 --> 00:33:02.880
how many people are trying
to drive up the price

00:33:02.880 --> 00:33:04.100
of these things,

00:33:04.100 --> 00:33:07.090
but anyone who is
manipulating the market here,

00:33:07.090 --> 00:33:10.690
really faces some truly
terrifying civil liability

00:33:10.690 --> 00:33:12.610
and maybe jail time.

00:33:12.610 --> 00:33:14.960
And those are just the on-chain problems,

00:33:14.960 --> 00:33:16.900
that takes us to the off-chain problems

00:33:16.900 --> 00:33:18.780
associated with NFTs.

00:33:18.780 --> 00:33:21.010
The first of which is
you need to make sure

00:33:21.010 --> 00:33:22.670
that if you're selling an NFT,

00:33:22.670 --> 00:33:25.710
you have the rights to the reference work.

00:33:25.710 --> 00:33:27.830
Recently, an NFT minter called Hitpiece,

00:33:27.830 --> 00:33:29.300
purported to issue an NFT

00:33:29.300 --> 00:33:33.500
related to every song
on Spotify's database.

00:33:33.500 --> 00:33:35.650
So they would effectively
take the album artwork

00:33:35.650 --> 00:33:40.540
and mint NFT related to the
artist and name of the song.

00:33:40.540 --> 00:33:44.270
It seems like that raises some
very truly terrifying issues

00:33:44.270 --> 00:33:47.270
in terms of Hitpiece not
owning any of the rights

00:33:47.270 --> 00:33:49.800
associated with any of the songs, artists,

00:33:49.800 --> 00:33:53.353
or album artwork associated with the NFTs

00:33:53.353 --> 00:33:55.470
that they claim to be making.

00:33:55.470 --> 00:33:56.303
Now, interestingly,

00:33:56.303 --> 00:33:59.130
there is a novel issue of copyright here.

00:33:59.130 --> 00:34:02.580
Since the NFT doesn't contain
the musical work itself,

00:34:02.580 --> 00:34:03.413
there's an open question

00:34:03.413 --> 00:34:07.100
whether these NFTs violate
the musical copyright.

00:34:07.100 --> 00:34:10.010
I'd argue they probably do,
but that's a separate issue.

00:34:10.010 --> 00:34:11.030
And on top of that,

00:34:11.030 --> 00:34:13.730
there is no question that minting an NFT

00:34:13.730 --> 00:34:16.910
related to a band and their song,

00:34:16.910 --> 00:34:19.550
using all of those names
and their album work,

00:34:19.550 --> 00:34:21.650
certainly violate the trademark

00:34:21.650 --> 00:34:24.560
related to this particular band and song

00:34:24.560 --> 00:34:26.240
and probably a myriad of other

00:34:26.240 --> 00:34:28.750
attribution related legal issues as well.

00:34:28.750 --> 00:34:31.310
Then there was another
music related NFT project,

00:34:31.310 --> 00:34:32.840
called Streamer.fm,

00:34:32.840 --> 00:34:33.740
that claimed that

00:34:33.740 --> 00:34:37.590
because you could stream
music on Spotify and YouTube,

00:34:37.590 --> 00:34:40.320
they were somehow allowed
to mint these NFTs

00:34:40.320 --> 00:34:41.800
and re-stream the music,

00:34:41.800 --> 00:34:44.740
which is not how any of this works at all.

00:34:44.740 --> 00:34:46.000
Fundamentally here,

00:34:46.000 --> 00:34:48.330
clearing the rights and making
sure you have the rights

00:34:48.330 --> 00:34:50.700
to mint the NFT in the first place,

00:34:50.700 --> 00:34:54.450
goes back to old fashioned
boring concepts of contract

00:34:54.450 --> 00:34:55.650
and copyright law.

00:34:55.650 --> 00:34:57.540
NFT and blockchain technology,

00:34:57.540 --> 00:35:00.590
not only doesn't supplant or replace

00:35:00.590 --> 00:35:02.760
those bedrock principles of law,

00:35:02.760 --> 00:35:05.950
they often run head long right into it.

00:35:05.950 --> 00:35:10.950
And when it comes to this Corpus
of Law and brand new NFTs,

00:35:11.626 --> 00:35:13.460
NFTs are gonna lose.

00:35:13.460 --> 00:35:18.040
But there's all kinds of
minting NFTs without permission.

00:35:18.040 --> 00:35:20.710
And often you have no way of knowing

00:35:20.710 --> 00:35:23.400
whether the original minter had the rights

00:35:23.400 --> 00:35:27.040
and the clearances to mint the
NFTs that they are minting.

00:35:27.040 --> 00:35:28.530
Proponents of NFTs claim

00:35:28.530 --> 00:35:31.280
that NFTs can prove digital provenance

00:35:31.280 --> 00:35:32.820
related to works of art,

00:35:32.820 --> 00:35:35.180
but the truth is often you have no idea

00:35:35.180 --> 00:35:37.620
if the original minter
has any of the rights

00:35:37.620 --> 00:35:39.680
associated with the
work in the first place.

00:35:39.680 --> 00:35:43.060
And there's absolutely no way to know,

00:35:43.060 --> 00:35:45.460
except through conducting an investigation

00:35:45.460 --> 00:35:47.140
completely off-chain.

00:35:47.140 --> 00:35:48.360
This is actually one of the issues

00:35:48.360 --> 00:35:51.890
associated with the Quintin
Tarantino and Miramax case.

00:35:51.890 --> 00:35:53.950
In that case, Quentin
Tarantino, the director,

00:35:53.950 --> 00:35:57.720
was trying to min some NFTs
related to film Pulp Fiction.

00:35:57.720 --> 00:36:00.310
And it's a complete miras
to try and figure this out

00:36:00.310 --> 00:36:02.750
because Tarantino retains some rights

00:36:02.750 --> 00:36:05.680
to the screenplay of
the movie Pulp Fiction,

00:36:05.680 --> 00:36:07.880
but Miramax retains
almost all of the rights

00:36:07.880 --> 00:36:09.620
associated with the movie itself.

00:36:09.620 --> 00:36:11.910
And it's going to take a lot of litigation

00:36:11.910 --> 00:36:14.010
to figure out who owns what rights

00:36:14.010 --> 00:36:15.779
and who has the right to make NFTs

00:36:15.779 --> 00:36:20.030
related to either the
screenplay or the movie itself.

00:36:20.030 --> 00:36:21.810
And good luck if you think the NFT itself

00:36:21.810 --> 00:36:25.080
is going to prove the answer
to either of those questions.

00:36:25.080 --> 00:36:25.913
And of course,

00:36:25.913 --> 00:36:28.470
there's all kinds of good old
fashioned regulatory risk.

00:36:28.470 --> 00:36:30.100
A lot of people have explained

00:36:30.100 --> 00:36:33.270
that an NFT or a blockchain
might make perfect sense

00:36:33.270 --> 00:36:36.010
in the context of say real estate title,

00:36:36.010 --> 00:36:40.400
where having a public
decentralized blockchain

00:36:40.400 --> 00:36:45.040
of who buys and sells houses
could actually be much better

00:36:45.040 --> 00:36:48.920
than the current system of
a centralized title system.

00:36:48.920 --> 00:36:51.861
But good luck trying to
convince a state or municipality

00:36:51.861 --> 00:36:53.100
to adopt that.

00:36:53.100 --> 00:36:55.330
And if your blockchain

00:36:55.330 --> 00:36:58.890
runs into something that's
already highly regulated,

00:36:58.890 --> 00:37:00.570
like real estate title,

00:37:00.570 --> 00:37:03.220
you can't wave a magic wand
and force the government

00:37:03.220 --> 00:37:05.560
to adopt your technical solution,

00:37:05.560 --> 00:37:09.800
even if your technical
solution has some benefits

00:37:09.800 --> 00:37:12.400
over the universally adopted system.

00:37:12.400 --> 00:37:13.820
One of the biggest issues is,

00:37:13.820 --> 00:37:15.190
as a downstream purchaser,

00:37:15.190 --> 00:37:16.810
how do you make sure that you can enforce

00:37:16.810 --> 00:37:19.310
all of the terms against
the previous per purchasers

00:37:19.310 --> 00:37:21.180
and the original NFT minter?

00:37:21.180 --> 00:37:24.270
But the problem also goes
the other way as well.

00:37:24.270 --> 00:37:27.670
If those terms do travel down that chain,

00:37:27.670 --> 00:37:31.720
let's say that you are a
band or a YouTube creator,

00:37:31.720 --> 00:37:33.660
and as part of your NFT,

00:37:33.660 --> 00:37:36.400
you issue some sort of unlockable content

00:37:36.400 --> 00:37:38.970
like a meet and greet with a band,

00:37:38.970 --> 00:37:41.100
and based on the terms of sale,

00:37:41.100 --> 00:37:44.780
anyone who ever purchases
that particular NFT

00:37:44.780 --> 00:37:48.370
has the right to enforce a meet
and greet against the band.

00:37:48.370 --> 00:37:49.690
Well, as the seller,

00:37:49.690 --> 00:37:51.870
are you prepared to offer a meet and greet

00:37:51.870 --> 00:37:53.150
with every single person

00:37:53.150 --> 00:37:56.240
who ever purchases that NFT down the line?

00:37:56.240 --> 00:37:59.070
Because depending on
the terms of the sale,

00:37:59.070 --> 00:38:00.940
you might be obligated to provide that.

00:38:00.940 --> 00:38:02.860
Otherwise you might get
hit with things like,

00:38:02.860 --> 00:38:04.940
breach of contract or fraud,

00:38:04.940 --> 00:38:06.990
depending on the value of the thing

00:38:06.990 --> 00:38:08.900
that you have promised to provide.

00:38:08.900 --> 00:38:11.090
I was talking to a very
large YouTube creator

00:38:11.090 --> 00:38:12.980
who wanted to create an NFT.

00:38:12.980 --> 00:38:14.120
I'm not going to name him,

00:38:14.120 --> 00:38:15.930
but I am going to wish him congratulations

00:38:15.930 --> 00:38:17.650
when he passes a hundred
billion subscribers

00:38:17.650 --> 00:38:18.940
in just a little while,

00:38:18.940 --> 00:38:21.840
and he wanted to offer a video conference

00:38:21.840 --> 00:38:25.880
with whoever purchased
this NFT 24 hours a day,

00:38:25.880 --> 00:38:28.550
365 days out of the year.

00:38:28.550 --> 00:38:31.120
And I suggested to him that
maybe that wasn't a good idea,

00:38:31.120 --> 00:38:33.860
unless he liked being woken
up in the middle of the night

00:38:33.860 --> 00:38:36.340
and being forced to
provide a video conference

00:38:36.340 --> 00:38:39.960
with every single person
who purchased his NFT.

00:38:39.960 --> 00:38:42.260
And lo and behold, he
didn't go through with it,

00:38:42.260 --> 00:38:43.780
and I think that was a smart thing.

00:38:43.780 --> 00:38:45.270
I've seen lots of people claim

00:38:45.270 --> 00:38:47.260
that there is absolutely no downside

00:38:47.260 --> 00:38:50.470
to creators creating their own NFTs.

00:38:50.470 --> 00:38:54.100
On the contrary, there's all
kinds of potential pitfalls

00:38:54.100 --> 00:38:56.200
if you not smart about these things.

00:38:56.200 --> 00:38:58.440
Then there's things like
product liabilities,

00:38:58.440 --> 00:38:59.520
NFTs break all the time.

00:38:59.520 --> 00:39:02.020
Who's responsible if
the link between the NFT

00:39:02.020 --> 00:39:03.940
and the reference material breaks?

00:39:03.940 --> 00:39:06.790
Who's responsible if the blockchain forks

00:39:06.790 --> 00:39:09.490
and your revenue stream is then cut off?

00:39:09.490 --> 00:39:12.790
These are bedrock ideas
of product liability,

00:39:12.790 --> 00:39:14.970
on top of which it's not at all clear,

00:39:14.970 --> 00:39:16.770
from the blockchain itself,

00:39:16.770 --> 00:39:18.530
that the thing isn't functioning.

00:39:18.530 --> 00:39:20.890
You've gotta do an
investigation off the blockchain

00:39:20.890 --> 00:39:22.600
to know if the thing
that's on the blockchain

00:39:22.600 --> 00:39:24.110
is actually working or not.

00:39:24.110 --> 00:39:26.100
There's been a lot of
talk about minting NFTs

00:39:26.100 --> 00:39:27.930
related to real world goods,

00:39:27.930 --> 00:39:30.180
creating an NFT related to a house

00:39:30.180 --> 00:39:32.290
to track who buys and sells the house,

00:39:32.290 --> 00:39:33.890
creating NFT related to a car,

00:39:33.890 --> 00:39:35.850
related to the car itself.

00:39:35.850 --> 00:39:38.940
But what happens when something
happens in the real world

00:39:38.940 --> 00:39:40.900
that isn't reflected on the blockchain?

00:39:40.900 --> 00:39:42.240
What happens if the house burns down?

00:39:42.240 --> 00:39:44.200
What happens if the car gets stolen?

00:39:44.200 --> 00:39:47.010
Rectifying real world events
with things on the blockchain

00:39:47.010 --> 00:39:50.310
is no easy task and is
inherently off-chain.

00:39:50.310 --> 00:39:52.580
And there are other
unintended consequences

00:39:52.580 --> 00:39:53.930
along with this as well.

00:39:53.930 --> 00:39:55.470
For example, Alfa Romeo just said

00:39:55.470 --> 00:39:56.900
that they're going to are offering NFTs

00:39:56.900 --> 00:39:58.170
related to their cars.

00:39:58.170 --> 00:40:00.840
And when you take your Alfa Romeo car

00:40:00.840 --> 00:40:03.600
into being serviced with Alfa Romeo,

00:40:03.600 --> 00:40:06.000
there will be a history on the blockchain

00:40:06.000 --> 00:40:08.320
that shows that your car was serviced

00:40:08.320 --> 00:40:10.550
at such and such a time
in such and such a way

00:40:10.550 --> 00:40:12.710
and that follows with the car.

00:40:12.710 --> 00:40:13.610
But one of the problems

00:40:13.610 --> 00:40:15.280
is what happens if you take your car

00:40:15.280 --> 00:40:17.310
to a non-authorized dealer,

00:40:17.310 --> 00:40:18.530
that information won't make it

00:40:18.530 --> 00:40:20.690
onto the Alfa Romeo blockchain.

00:40:20.690 --> 00:40:22.780
It's one way that Alfa
Romeo might force you

00:40:22.780 --> 00:40:24.740
to use their walled garden.

00:40:24.740 --> 00:40:26.570
It'd be like DRM for a car,

00:40:26.570 --> 00:40:28.450
you'd never be able to take your car

00:40:28.450 --> 00:40:30.840
to any other service place.

00:40:30.840 --> 00:40:32.330
Now, I am not a computer programmer,

00:40:32.330 --> 00:40:33.490
these might be technical problems

00:40:33.490 --> 00:40:35.080
that will be fixed over time.

00:40:35.080 --> 00:40:37.930
Already we're seeing
intermediaries like OpenSea,

00:40:37.930 --> 00:40:40.240
interpret the data on blockchain

00:40:40.240 --> 00:40:42.010
and solve some of the issues

00:40:42.010 --> 00:40:45.360
when it comes to falsely minted NFTs.

00:40:45.360 --> 00:40:46.460
But if that's the case

00:40:46.460 --> 00:40:49.580
and we need these intermediaries
that are off-chain,

00:40:49.580 --> 00:40:50.540
it raises the question

00:40:50.540 --> 00:40:52.930
Why would we want Web 3?

00:40:52.930 --> 00:40:56.600
In fact, we can do all
of this stuff legally

00:40:56.600 --> 00:40:59.670
using traditional websites and
traditional legal mechanisms.

00:40:59.670 --> 00:41:03.230
Often the Web 3 part of it
just makes things worse.

00:41:03.230 --> 00:41:06.190
Which takes us to the third
category of problems with NFTs,

00:41:06.190 --> 00:41:08.290
which I've called innovation so mundane

00:41:08.290 --> 00:41:09.700
that no one should care.

00:41:09.700 --> 00:41:10.660
And the issue here

00:41:10.660 --> 00:41:14.150
is that there are probably
plenty of marginal benefits

00:41:14.150 --> 00:41:15.550
that NFTs bring to the table

00:41:15.550 --> 00:41:17.480
for a wide variety of industries,

00:41:17.480 --> 00:41:18.620
they're just not huge wins

00:41:18.620 --> 00:41:21.250
that are going to create an
enormous amount of wealth

00:41:21.250 --> 00:41:23.490
for the people with vested interests.

00:41:23.490 --> 00:41:27.540
It's pretty clear that
collectible JPEGs are pretty dumb

00:41:27.540 --> 00:41:29.640
and that this is a speculative bubble

00:41:29.640 --> 00:41:33.930
with untold numbers of non
arm length transactions,

00:41:33.930 --> 00:41:36.050
wash sales and sock puppets.

00:41:36.050 --> 00:41:37.010
It's pretty clear at this point

00:41:37.010 --> 00:41:39.130
that at least this
first generation of NFTs

00:41:39.130 --> 00:41:41.030
are creating a speculative bubble

00:41:41.030 --> 00:41:43.940
that is probably going to
burst pretty violently.

00:41:43.940 --> 00:41:45.090
Collectible JPEGs

00:41:45.090 --> 00:41:48.150
and other collectible
digital pieces of artwork,

00:41:48.150 --> 00:41:51.050
it's probably not going to last.

00:41:51.050 --> 00:41:52.290
It's pretty dumb

00:41:52.290 --> 00:41:54.340
and I think people will
probably get bored with it.

00:41:54.340 --> 00:41:56.560
It's also filled with all kinds

00:41:56.560 --> 00:41:58.660
of non arm length transactions,

00:41:58.660 --> 00:42:01.890
wash sales and sock puppets
that are propping up the prices

00:42:01.890 --> 00:42:05.200
and just waiting for another
person to come around

00:42:05.200 --> 00:42:08.940
and buy the thing that probably
isn't worth the sales price.

00:42:08.940 --> 00:42:09.773
But that being said,

00:42:09.773 --> 00:42:11.180
there are probably lots of other uses

00:42:11.180 --> 00:42:12.280
that are more promising,

00:42:12.280 --> 00:42:13.640
fractionalized revenue streams,

00:42:13.640 --> 00:42:16.200
access to unique membership
style opportunities,

00:42:16.200 --> 00:42:19.830
proving certain kinds of
Providence and raising equity.

00:42:19.830 --> 00:42:24.290
But the thing is those
uses of NFTs are genuine,

00:42:24.290 --> 00:42:26.090
but they're really just
marginal improvements

00:42:26.090 --> 00:42:27.380
on current technology,

00:42:27.380 --> 00:42:29.360
if they are indeed improvements at all.

00:42:29.360 --> 00:42:33.120
And NFT acolytes are claiming
NFTs are a grand slam,

00:42:33.120 --> 00:42:34.040
but it's probably probably more

00:42:34.040 --> 00:42:36.270
like a simple walk on single.

00:42:36.270 --> 00:42:39.220
These improvements in
technology are probably useful,

00:42:39.220 --> 00:42:41.460
but they're also extremely mundane.

00:42:41.460 --> 00:42:42.660
The technology at use here

00:42:42.660 --> 00:42:45.210
probably will form some
of the unseen backbone

00:42:45.210 --> 00:42:46.870
of certain transactions,

00:42:46.870 --> 00:42:49.950
but it just simply won't
be get rich quick schemes.

00:42:49.950 --> 00:42:51.890
It seems likely that the huge market

00:42:51.890 --> 00:42:54.890
for NFTs as collectibles will
probably crash at some point.

00:42:54.890 --> 00:42:57.980
And when the bubble around JPEGs dies out,

00:42:57.980 --> 00:42:59.990
what will be left is probably a technology

00:42:59.990 --> 00:43:02.510
that makes marginal improvements.

00:43:02.510 --> 00:43:04.230
It's going to create a few millionaires,

00:43:04.230 --> 00:43:05.310
maybe even billionaires

00:43:05.310 --> 00:43:09.370
who find novel and practical
uses for this technology,

00:43:09.370 --> 00:43:11.470
but it's not going to create
overnight millionaires

00:43:11.470 --> 00:43:13.290
for everyone that wants to mint

00:43:13.290 --> 00:43:15.980
some low value NFT going forward.

00:43:15.980 --> 00:43:18.430
And the reason that that
is a problem for NFTs,

00:43:18.430 --> 00:43:20.010
is that there are many, many people

00:43:20.010 --> 00:43:21.450
who have staked their entire reputations,

00:43:21.450 --> 00:43:23.300
as well as their entire finances,

00:43:23.300 --> 00:43:25.940
on the proposition that NFTs have created

00:43:25.940 --> 00:43:27.750
a completely new industry

00:43:27.750 --> 00:43:31.160
and that it can create overnight
millionaires in anyone.

00:43:31.160 --> 00:43:33.190
And when people realize
that that's not the case,

00:43:33.190 --> 00:43:35.890
well, the entire industry
may evaporate a overnight,

00:43:35.890 --> 00:43:37.550
faster than Beanie Babies.

00:43:37.550 --> 00:43:38.383
Now, of course,

00:43:38.383 --> 00:43:41.250
NFTs are heralded as this
revolution in creator economics,

00:43:41.250 --> 00:43:44.230
as a way for artists to actually
paid what they're worth,

00:43:44.230 --> 00:43:46.662
but people don't realize
that there's real liability

00:43:46.662 --> 00:43:49.110
and that NFTs could be
incredibly dangerous

00:43:49.110 --> 00:43:51.650
if not outright terrible for creators.

00:43:51.650 --> 00:43:54.100
I go into great detail based
on my experience as a lawyer,

00:43:54.100 --> 00:43:56.170
but also my experience
as a creator myself.

00:43:56.170 --> 00:43:57.003
Let's just say,

00:43:57.003 --> 00:43:59.960
there's a reason I haven't
minted any NFTs myself.

00:43:59.960 --> 00:44:01.560
Now, what if I told you
that you could watch

00:44:01.560 --> 00:44:02.620
another LegalEagle video,

00:44:02.620 --> 00:44:05.330
but this time it's on why
NFTs could be dangerous

00:44:05.330 --> 00:44:07.160
and bad for creators.

00:44:07.160 --> 00:44:09.400
Well, this full length
companion video does exist

00:44:09.400 --> 00:44:11.660
and it is exclusively on Nebula.

00:44:11.660 --> 00:44:12.950
Because sadly, if I put it on YouTube,

00:44:12.950 --> 00:44:14.547
it would probably get demonetized

00:44:14.547 --> 00:44:16.500
and there's no way you'd ever see it.

00:44:16.500 --> 00:44:17.790
But Nebula is different.

00:44:17.790 --> 00:44:18.790
As you've probably heard by now,

00:44:18.790 --> 00:44:21.460
Nebula is home to tons of
exclusive ad free content,

00:44:21.460 --> 00:44:24.220
like my first full length
documentary, Bad Law Words Good,

